  "answer": "CDK5-dependent NUP98 Ser606 phosphorylation initiates transport failure in TARDBP-mutant motor neurons\n\nHypothesis. In TARDBP-mutant spinal motor neurons, CDK5-dependent NUP98 Ser606 phosphorylation weakens NUP98–NUP96 anchoring, reducing pore retention and active nuclear import before persistent cytoplasmic TDP-43 assemblies appear. Import failure then promotes nuclear TDP-43 depletion, assemblies, and neuronal dysfunction; assemblies subsequently amplify transport failure. “Initiating” means preceding import failure and persistent assemblies, not being the mutation’s first consequence. This causal chain is speculative and restricted to the selected genetic context.\n\nSupported premises. Disease-linked TDP-43 disrupts nucleoporin localization and transport, with related pore pathology in ALS tissue [S1]. Persistent TDP-43 assemblies can impair import and accompany nuclear depletion and cell death without conventional stress granules [S3]. Stress granules provide a separate transport-factor-sequestration route [S4]. C9orf72-associated import defects establish another context-specific route, not this mechanism [S2]. The packet supports assembly-to-transport causation more directly than the proposed reverse direction; phosphorylated TDP-43 does not establish phosphorylated NUP98.\n\nProvisional nomination and validation. Choosing NUP98 and assigning NUP96 an anchoring role rely on unverified prior knowledge outside the packet. CDK5–Ser606 selection lacks packet-supported biological justification; phosphorylation-induced conformational weakening of their anchoring interface is an explicit assumption. Before prioritization, confirm residue numbering in the expressed isoform, endogenous neuronal Ser606 phosphorylation by site-resolved mass spectrometry, and direct phosphorylation of purified full-length NUP98 by active CDK5, using kinase-inactive and S606A controls. Acute CDK5 depletion with wild-type versus kinase-dead rescue must change endogenous site occupancy before injury. Compare phosphorylated and unmodified NUP98 binding to NUP96: reduced affinity is the specified molecular prediction. Failed validation rejects this nomination rather than licensing an unsupported substitute.\n\nTemporal and necessity tests. Use patient-derived mutant motor neurons, isogenic corrected controls, independent clones, and differentiation batches. Pair longitudinal imaging with matched phosphoproteomics to measure occupancy normalized to total NUP98, pore retention, import kinetics, nuclear TDP-43, persistent assemblies, stress granules, transport-factor localization, axonal integrity, and survival. Require phosphorylation and retention loss before import slowing, assemblies, or generalized injury. Test endogenous S606A alongside synonymous edits and wild-type reversion, checking baseline abundance, pore function, and maturation. Orthogonal CDK5 inhibition must reduce Ser606 occupancy to demonstrate pathway engagement. Concordant protection and nonadditivity support a shared pathway only after excluding ceiling effects; chronology alone is insufficient.\n\nOrthogonal sufficiency and mediation. Potential structural effects of S606A and multisubstrate effects of kinase recruitment require a modification-specific test. Establish functional full-length NUP98 replacement in motor-neuron-derived nuclei, then compare otherwise identical proteins carrying authentic Ser606 phosphoserine or unmodified serine. Include a dephosphorylated preparation; verify folding, phosphorylation stoichiometry, and absence of other modifications. Measure NUP96 binding, pore residence separately from initial incorporation, and import with identical soluble transport factors. Phosphomimetics are not equivalent evidence. To test whether retention loss mediates import failure, engineer an inducible NUP98–NUP96 tether outside Ser606. Restoring retention while preserving verified phosphorylation should restore import. Require baseline-neutral tether behavior, inactive-tether controls, normal passive permeability, and envelope integrity. Persistent import failure despite restored retention rejects this mediation claim.\n\nAssembly-first alternatives. Stress-granule suppression alone cannot test the granule-independent route [S3]. Develop two anti-demixing perturbations—an engineered assembly-blocking intrabody and a demixing-reduced TDP-43 replacement retaining the disease mutation—and cross each with S606A after independently validating assembly suppression. Require preserved TDP-43 abundance, RNA-processing function, and baseline import, with no direct CDK5/NUP98 effects in assembly-free controls. Separately suppress stress-granule assembly. If preventing persistent assemblies eliminates early phosphorylation and import failure, assembly-first causation is favored. If both persist while later injury improves, assemblies are more consistent with downstream amplification. Standardized-soluble-factor import assays additionally distinguish retained pore dysfunction from soluble-factor sequestration. ATP, viability, envelope integrity, and passive-permeability controls address generalized injury.\n\nFalsification and limitations. Verified elimination of Ser606 phosphorylation, with normal baseline pore function, that leaves early NUP98 displacement and import impairment unchanged would falsify phosphorylation necessity. Phosphorylation appearing only after transport failure contradicts initiation. Limitations include difficult authentic-phosphoprotein replacement, altered behavior from editing or tethering, pleiotropic assembly interventions, undetected early assemblies or stress, immature cultures, and uncertain relevance beyond TARDBP-mutant ALS. Passing these tests would support a preclinical mechanism, not establish an ALS treatment.",
